  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 140 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nBy Friday, the upper level trough across the Great Lakes is expected \nto become a cut off low, as the Pacific trough attempts to track \nfurther inland. This will keep the upper level ridging for Friday, \nwith fairly rinse and repeat temperatures across the region. \nHowever, could see some gusty winds across the region Friday \nafternoon, with peak gusts around 30 mph. However, minimum humidity \nvalues are expected to remain above 50 percent (especially given the \ncooler temperatures), so do not expect this to turn into elevated \nfire weather conditions at this time. \n\nBy the weekend, the upper level pattern is expected to get more \ncomplicated. The upper level low is expected to slowly track across \nthe Great Lakes, with the Pacific trough turning into a split flow \nregime. In the split flow regime, expecting a shortwave to eject \nacross the southern Plains, with a second shortwave tracking into \nthe northern Intermountain West. The southern shortwave is expected \nto help a low form across the southern Plains, bringing \nprecipitation chances across Kansas and Oklahoma, with guidance \nsuggesting southwest into central Nebraska getting clipped by the \nnorthern edge for a few hours. Overall, chances of precipitation \nremain on the lower end, with highest potential remaining south of \nInterstate 80. Latest ensemble guidance generally keeps a 30 percent \nchance for measurable precipitation (with a 40 to 50 percent chance \nsouth of Interstate 80) across the region. However, when looking at \nprobabilities of greater than one tenth, percentages are less than \n10 percent. All this to say, though there are precipitation chances \nthis weekend, expect very little precipitation will fall if we \ndo get clipped by this system, with a few hundredths at most. \nSomething to keep an eye on for the northernmost extent of the \nprecipitation shield is the potential for some light snow showers\novernight Saturday across portions of the Sandhills. However, \nwith very little precipitation expected, also expect very little\nto no accumulations if snow were to develop. However, cooler \nair continues to advect into the region, favoring highs in the \n40s this weekend across most of the region, with lows remaining \nin the lower 30s. These forecast highs remain just cooler than \nour typical highs around 50 degrees. However, lows are expected \nto remain warmer than average, as typical lows are in the lower 20s.\n\nEarly to mid next week, a bit of uncertainty begins to arise behind \nthe split flow pattern. Guidance remains fairly high confidence in \nridging returning briefly on Monday, favoring a slight warm up back \nto seasonal highs in the 50s. This push of warm advection may also \nusher in gusty southerly winds, so will continue to keep an eye on \nthis period. The uncertainty really picks up by Tuesday, as long \nrange guidance shows disagreement on the placement of another \nPacific trough. The European solution remains slightly more \nprogressive, bringing the trough further east than the GFS solution. \nFor Tuesday, this should mostly favor seasonal highs with more \npotential for gusty winds across the region. By Wednesday, the \ndisparity in solutions brings some cause for concern, as this may \nhave an impact on precipitation chances as well as temperatures. If \nthe slower GFS solution were to occur, precipitation chances remain \nsparse, with warmer temperatures possible. However, if the European \nsolution occurs, then we may see cooler temperatures as well as a \nchance for precipitation. At this range, will continue to keep an \neye on the forecast trends, as high uncertainty remains.",
